--TEST--
`null as string` is the empty string, and the static `null`, a `?string` holding one and `echo` all agree — ADR 0007 § 2
--FILE--
<?nvs
// ADR 0007 § 2's "anything → `string`" row at `null`. PHP renders it as the
// empty string, and a `?string` holding one already did here through
// `Helper::TaggedToString`, so the *statically* `null` operand — the same
// value one type earlier — renders the same rather than being refused. The
// three spellings below are asserted to agree, not each to print something
// plausible on its own.
?string $absent = null;
string $fromTagged = $absent as string;
string $fromLiteral = null as string;

echo "[", $fromLiteral, "]\n";
echo "[", $fromTagged, "]\n";
// `echo` is the implicit site, and `E0707`'s doc is the one home for why
// `null` is not among the four types it refuses.
echo "[", null, "]\n";
echo ($fromLiteral == $fromTagged) as string, "\n";
--EXPECT--
[]
[]
[]
1
